      {
        "id": "the-whole-disaster-started-with-an-adblue-warning-light",
        "title": "The whole disaster started with an AdBlue warning light.",
        "description": "The owner topped up AdBlue, but the warning didn't clear. Repeated limp mode events followed. Each reset bought time, but carbon contamination worsened until the engine needed replacement.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "/case-studies/assets/1.2-peugeot-boxer-p22dte-limp-mode-nc500.png",
            "alt": "Peugeot Boxer motorhome in limp mode on NC500 Scotland with engine warning",
            "width": "",
            "height": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "What owner thought"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "“Just top up AdBlue”"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The motorhome still drove, so the symptom did not feel urgent."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "What it actually was"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "EGR clogged + NOx sensor failed"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Carbon build-up prevented proper regens, leading to extensive internal contamination."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Why it matters"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Engine replacement"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Once carbon has contaminated pistons, rings, and turbo, repair often costs more than replacement."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If your motorhome diesel develops an AdBlue warning that does not clear within 50 miles of refilling, have the NOx sensors and AdBlue system diagnosed immediately. Ignoring repeated limp mode events can lead to extensive carbon contamination and engine replacement."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Failure timeline"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "source": {
          "sectionId": "the-whole-disaster-started-with-an-adblue-warning-light",
          "order": 1
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "from-adblue-warning-to-engine-replacement-in-just-3-000-miles",
        "title": "From AdBlue warning to engine replacement in just 3,000 miles.",
        "description": "This case is a classic motorhome emissions-system failure: the owner ignored the warning signs and paid the price.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "/case-studies/assets/2.2-peugeot-boxer-p22dte-timeline.png",
            "alt": "Peugeot Boxer P22DTE timeline from AdBlue warnings to engine replacement",
            "width": "",
            "height": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "What the owner noticed"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "AdBlue warning at 82k, repeated limp mode events, countdown timer, and permanent power loss at 85k."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "What was happening internally"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "EGR clogged, NOx sensor failed, carbon built up on pistons and rings, causing low compression and turbo contamination."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "“The mechanic said, ‘Look, I can strip your engine down and see if it’s salvageable. That’s £700 in labour before I’ve even ordered a single part. Or I can fit a reconditioned engine for £3,600 all-in with a warranty. Which sounds smarter to you-’ He was right. The reconditioned engine has been perfect for 10,000 miles.”"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "82,000 miles"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "AdBlue warning appears"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "85,000 miles"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Permanent limp mode on NC500"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Specialist"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Reconditioned engine fitted"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Workshop diagnosis"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "source": {
          "sectionId": "from-adblue-warning-to-engine-replacement-in-just-3-000-miles",
          "order": 2
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "the-egr-was-clogged-the-nox-sensor-had-failed-and-carbon-had-contaminated-the-engine",
        "title": "The EGR was clogged, the NOx sensor had failed, and carbon had contaminated the engine.",
        "description": "The specialist read fault codes, performed a compression test, and borescoped the engine. The findings revealed extensive carbon contamination and low compression on two cylinders.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "/case-studies/assets/3.1-peugeot-boxer-p22dte-egr-carbon.png",
            "alt": "Peugeot P22DTE EGR valve clogged with carbon causing emissions failure",
            "width": "",
            "height": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "/case-studies/assets/3.2-peugeot-boxer-p22dte-carbon-buildup.png",
            "alt": "Peugeot P22DTE engine showing heavy carbon build-up on pistons and valves",
            "width": "",
            "height": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "/case-studies/assets/3.3-peugeot-boxer-p22dte-nox-sensor.png",
            "alt": "Peugeot P22DTE NOx sensor failure causing AdBlue system malfunction",
            "width": "",
            "height": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "NOx sensor"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Failed — generating incorrect readings"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "EGR valve"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Clogged with carbon deposits — stuck partially open"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "AdBlue system"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Multiple logged faults"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Compression test"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Low on two cylinders"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Borescope"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Heavy carbon build-up on piston crowns and cylinder walls"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Turbocharger"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Signs of carbon debris contamination"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Oil condition"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Thick, black, contaminated"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Specialist summary"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "“The motorhome has sat idle for long periods between trips. The EGR system has clogged with carbon. The NOx sensor failed, which meant the engine management couldn’t properly manage regeneration cycles. The repeated limp mode events were a symptom, but now we’re looking at extensive carbon contamination, low compression, and a turbo that may fail soon. The cost of repairing this engine properly exceeds the cost of fitting a reconditioned exchange unit. A replacement is the more economical option.”"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Repair options"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "source": {
          "sectionId": "the-egr-was-clogged-the-nox-sensor-had-failed-and-carbon-had-contaminated-the-engine",
          "order": 3
        }
